{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqfl5llz4rj","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Gate KB note promotion in the drain, not inline — usage attribution against a pinned snapshot","summary":"SkillOpt-derived design for adding validation-gate discipline to KB learning WITHOUT per-note cost. SkillOpt's gate is training-time, not runtime: notes drop cheaply, the gate is amortized into a batch pass. Three tiers: (1) RUNTIME admit cheap — provisional [ingest-candidate], gated only by the existing 0.70 dup guard + cosine, never bench on the hot path; (2) FREE implicit-lift signal — every judge verdict already produced is a labeled datapoint (notes A,B,C were RAG-recalled into a task context; outcome was approve/kickback), so attribute credit/blame to recalled notes via counterfactual logging, extending the existing shadow-journal flywheel to per-note attribution with zero extra runs; (3) OFFLINE batch gate (the SkillOpt 'training pass') — the learner drain periodically promotes provisional→durable only for notes whose accumulated attributed lift clears a bar and demotes negative-lift notes, per batch not per note, and any real bench runs once per drain. This directly resolves the non-reproducibility flagged in note-mqbbndrgd1k: the batch pass pins a KB SNAPSHOT (tag/hash) and benches against the frozen snapshot, so the live KB stays a moving target for cheap recall while the EVALUATED KB is always frozen — exactly why SkillOpt deploys a frozen best_skill.md. Borrow SkillOpt's propose-and-test discipline + rejected-edit failure buffer; do NOT copy its strict 'accept only if score strictly improves' gate literally — the orchestrator faces an open drifting task stream (no stable held-out split), so the conditional/rolling boundary is the correct adaptation.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-15T19:07:56.711Z","vec":[-0.1336677074432373,0.0013760311994701624,-0.030897049233317375,0.03664374351501465,0.044287096709012985,0.0198662132024765,0.0446480400860309